+We study retrieval, reranker, and selection baselines. The retrievers include sparse and dense retrievers \citep{su2026skillretrieval} and SkillSight, a hybrid retriever. The retrievers use fixed pretrained encoders and/or lexical scoring, with no training on our task-training split, and remain unchanged across task partitions. SkillRouter likewise uses its released reranker checkpoint without fine-tuning on our task-training split. LLM Select is a static router, and LLM Direct and LLM Oracle are downstream performance baselines representing without skills and with ground-truth skill set, respectively \citep{su2026skillretrieval}. The non-parametric optimization baselines are SkillDag and Skill-Pro \citep{pmlr-v306-mi26d,bai2026skilldag}. $\mathrm{RouterBSI}_{\mathrm{init}}$ and RouterBSI refer to the cold-start and final iteration performance. $\mathrm{RouterBSI}_{\mathrm{strict}}$ selects skills only when their predicate evaluates to true. $\mathrm{RouterBSI}_{\mathrm{top}k}$ are the top k skills using the sum of their predicate label and the normalized retrieval score.
